## 7. Industry context (per segment)

**CMP slurry and colloidal silica (EM).**
- Market size: CMP slurry about ¥300 bn in 2025 rising to about ¥420 bn in 2030 (chart reading of MTP P15, axis in oku; approximate), value CAGR 6.5%; volume CAGR 5-6%; source Fuji Chimera / Fuji Keizai as processed by Fuso. Fuso EM sales are therefore roughly 13% of the slurry market value [I: 415/3,100; EM includes non-CMP items].
- Concentration: ND. Slurry formulators are customers; abrasive suppliers named in the folder: Fuso and Nissan Chemical (Snowtex). Other abrasive makers and substitutes [I-general, to research].
- Commercial model: line-by-line customer qualification, then long-term supply; annual-type price negotiation, with cost-sharing for new capacity from FY03/2027 [D: Dial P5].
- Growth phase: demand-led, capacity constrained until the Kyoto plant (Feb-2029); the integrated report lists "capacity constraint versus demand" as an internal risk [D: IR25 P24].
- Margin structure: EM OP margin 30-48% over FY03/2021-26; depreciation 6-22% of EM sales.

**Fruit acids and food additives (LS).**
- Market size and concentration: ND in the folder. Facts: Fuso is a rare domestic fruit-acid maker; overseas share above 50% of LS sales; competition from overseas makers, especially Chinese low-cost products, is intensifying [D: MTP P11; Yuho26 P14].
- Commercial model: tenders and price lists; raw-material-linked price revisions; share-vs-price trade-offs (fumaric price index 0.91) [D: Deck26 P21-22].
- Growth: mature; plan CAGR 4.0% sales, 7.2% OP to FY03/2031.

**Why the segments behave differently.** EM: qualification, capital intensity, chip cycle, concentration. LS: tenders, commodity-linked inputs, consumer and industrial demand, foreign competition (see Deep-dive section 5.3).

